issues.issue:
Lexical gaps cause false abstentions on natural phrasings:
- 'Rex is my dog.' (a single sentence-initial name with no mid-sentence repeat);
- names and places written in lowercase, as in chat text;
- bare amounts with no currency ('Rent went up to 1,450 a month');
- 'biweekly';
- occupations given as verbs ('Mum teaches piano', 'codes for a bank', 'is in IT');
- 'from 2015 to 2019' for 'how long did I live';
- semicolon sentence splits that separate a name from its relation word.
The held-out false-abstention rate is still low (about 1.5%), so these matter mainly for user-written stores.

The filler detectors are too permissive, so many unanswerables pass as filled:
- any new capitalised word fills a name or entity slot (places, nationalities, 'Uncle Joe', 'Central Park', 'Thai', 'Glasgow' for 'which school');
- 'a' and 'an' are in the number-word list, so 'twice a week' fills a 'how long' slot;
- '8:30' fills duration;
- 'free of turbulence' fills money;
- 'always' fills frequency;
- any occupation word fills occupation ('fan of the director Nolan', 'keen baker').
This is the same structural reason the rule cannot catch entity-swap unanswerables.
